body:
A subclass replacing a [[Method]] it inherited from its parent. Same name, same parameters,
different body.

```c++
class Animal {
public:
	virtual void speak() { cout << "..."; }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
	void speak() { cout << "Woof"; }    // overrides Animal::speak
};
```

Not to be confused with [[Overloading]], which is the one people mix it up with:

| | [[Overloading]] | Overriding |
|---|---|---|
| Signatures | **different** parameters | **identical** |
| Needs inheritance | no | yes |
| Resolved | at compile time, by the argument types | at run time, by what the object is |
| Lives in | one class, or none | a subclass replacing its parent |

The run-time half needs `virtual` on the base class method. Without it, calling through an
`Animal*` that points at a `Dog` runs `Animal::speak`, because the compiler picks by the
pointer's declared type. With it, the decision waits until the program is running and picks by
the actual object. That is polymorphism, and it is the payoff for the whole inheritance
arrangement.

[[Class Relationships]] has the is-a / has-a decision this sits on top of. [[Classes]] ·
[[Copying]] · [[Object Oriented Approach]] in [[INTRODUCTION TO SOFTWARE ENGINEERING]].
